Equivalence Groups Found
└── Modbus TCP client for reading/writing holding registers to a PLC/field device
    ├── Candidates : pymodbus, modbus-tk, minimalmodbus
    ├── Used APIs  : ModbusTcpClient, client.connect, client.read_holding_registers, client.write_register, rr.isError, rr.registers
    ├── Pitfalls   : pymodbus returns response objects with .isError() and .registers; modbus-tk uses execute() with function-code constants and raises 
    │   exceptions on error instead of returning error objects; minimalmodbus's read_register/write_register API assumes serial connections by default and 
    │   doesn't expose 'unit'/slave id the same way pymodbus does with TCP; Unit/slave addressing conventions differ between libraries (unit= kwarg vs 
    │   slaveaddress parameter); Connection lifecycle differs: pymodbus needs explicit .connect(), minimalmodbus opens serial port on instantiation; Error 
    │   handling model differs significantly: exception-based (modbus-tk, minimalmodbus) vs return-object-based (pymodbus), requiring rewritten error checks in
    │   ModbusIO._read1 and write_valve
    ├── Constraints: requires_connector
    └── All three libraries provide Modbus protocol clients capable of reading/writing holding registers over TCP or serial, which is the exact role played 
        here (reading temperature/pressure registers, writing a valve setpoint register). pymodbus is the most TCP-native and closely matches the current usage
        pattern (dedicated ModbusTcpClient class, read_holding_registers/write_register calls returning response objects with isError()). modbus-tk also 
        supports TCP master connections with comparable read/write primitives, though its API is execute()-based rather than method-per-function-code. 
        minimalmodbus is primarily designed for serial (RTU/ASCII) communication and has a much simpler single-register read_register/write_register API 
        without the same TCP client abstraction or response-object error model, so while conceptually equivalent it requires significant glue code and may not 
        natively support TCP transport the same way.

